<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hereford</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hereford is a cathedral city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-wye">River Wye</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, the county town of Herefordshire. A bishopric was established there in the late 7th century for the Magonsaete, and the diocese long kept the outline of their kingdom. Its cathedral holds the Hereford Mappa Mundi of about 1300 and a chained library.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hereford.jpg" alt="Hereford" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clint Mann · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centre_of_Hereford_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4306743.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ralph Vaughan Williams wrote a descant for the hymnal Songs of Praise (1925) and an orchestral prelude on Eventide for the <b>Hereford</b> Festival of 1936, and Charles Ives set the hymn around 1890.</blockquote><a href="/songs/abide-with-me">Abide with Me</a></li><li><blockquote>A Scottish Covenanter army under Alexander Leslie, Earl of Leven, laid siege to <b>Hereford</b>, one of the remaining Royalist strongholds of the English Civil War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-30">30 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A bishopric at <b>Hereford</b> served the people.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-magonsaete">Kingdom of the Magonsaete</a></li><li><blockquote>It passed by marriage to the de Braose family and then to the de Bohuns, earls of <b>Hereford</b>, and later to the Staffords, dukes of Buckingham.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-brecknock">Lordship of Brecknock</a></li></ul></section>
